$$
When $ J=0 $, $ \Omega = 2|\Delta| $, but $ J=0 $, so numerator $ 4J^2 = 0 $, so $ P=0 $. This matches, even if $ \Delta=0 $, since numerator vanishes. If $ J=0 $ and $ \Delta=0 $, $ H = K Z\otimes Z $, still diagonal, $ |01\rangle $ is eigenvector, so no transition. So the formula gives $ P=0 $ when $ J=0 $, correct. This is an algebraic identity: the expression for $ P $ has a factor $ J^2 $, so vanishes when $ J=0 $, consistent with decoupled dynamics.

\rho_1(t) = \begin{pmatrix}
|a|^2 & a b^* \\
a^* b & |b|^2
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
\cos^2(\Omega t) + \frac{K^2}{\Omega^2} \sin^2(\Omega t) & -\frac{2JK}{\Omega^2} \sin^2(\Omega t) + i \frac{2J}{\Omega} \sin(\Omega t)\cos(\Omega t) \\
-\frac{2JK}{\Omega^2} \sin^2(\Omega t) - i \frac{2J}{\Omega} \sin(\Omega t)\cos(\Omega t) & \frac{4J^2}{\Omega^2} \sin^2(\Omega t)
\end{pmatrix}.
$$

This is the reduced density matrix of the first spin.

